# The table of `o200k_base`, the encoding of GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, GPT-5 and the
# models o1, o3 and o4-mini, alone: a program that uses this module carries
# this table and no other, where one that uses `tokenizer/tables`, which also
# finds an encoding by its name or its model, carries all three. Measured
# 2026-11-18 on this Mac at -O2, a program that counts two words is 2.05 MB
# with `tokenizer/tables/cl100k_base`, 3.42 MB with
# `tokenizer/tables/o200k_base`, 4.97 MB through `tokenizer/tables`, and
# 0.81 MB with no table. What an encoding does is `tokenizer`'s. Nothing
# here fails.

use tokenizer
use tokenizer/tables/internal/made as making
use tokenizer/tables/internal/o200k_base as o200k

# The encoding `o200k_base`: 199,998 ordinary tokens and the special tokens
# `<|endoftext|>` (199999) and `<|endofprompt|>` (200018).
#
# It reads the whole table; make it once and keep it. See also
# `tokenizer.encode_ordinary`.
function encoding() -> tokenizer.Encoding
    return making.made(
        name: "o200k_base",
        pattern: o200k.PATTERN,
        ranks: o200k.RANKS,
        texts: o200k.SPECIAL_TEXTS,
        numbers: o200k.SPECIAL_TOKENS
    )

## Examples

# Two words counted and encoded in `o200k_base`.
test "encoding: two words"
    enc = encoding()
    assert enc.name == "o200k_base"
    assert tokenizer.encode_ordinary(enc, text: "hello world") == [24912, 2375]
